cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this research, please cite the software and working paper."
title: "Epistemic Amputation: Distinguishing Persistent Belief from Readable but Non-Causal State in Learned Agents"
type: software
license: MIT
authors:
  - given-names: "James Siyuan"
    family-names: "He"
version: 0.1.1
date-released: 2026-09-01
repository-code: "https://github.com/Silentpartnercoding/epistemic-amputation"
url: "https://silentpartnercoding.github.io/epistemic-amputation/"
abstract: >-
  Preregistered causal assays testing whether evidence-earned internal
  representations persist after decisive counterevidence and continue to
  control model decisions. The final Gemma 4/J-space verdict is NOT SUPPORTED;
  the principal result is a dissociation between readable internal content and
  causal control.
keywords:
  - mechanistic interpretability
  - belief revision
  - activation patching
  - Jacobian lens
  - J-space
  - causal inference
  - language models
